# Before: ordinary existential witnesses compiled, but the convenient
# `witness $P(args)` statement was rejected even though its Result retained the
# concrete definition, argument checks, instantiated existential, and ordinary
# witness verification.
#
# Now: the statement compiles the ordinary witness verification in a child
# environment, folds that proof through the already compiled predicate, and
# publishes three exact results: `$has_copy(2)`, `2 $in R`, and
# `exist x R st {x = 2}` under their retained FactIds.
#
# Boundary: this tracer uses one ordinary witness and one existential body
# fact. `exist!`, multiple witnesses, and non-object predicate parameters stay
# fail-closed until their distinct Result shapes have reviewed adapters. A
# focused Rust test rejects a missing inferred existential FactId.
prop has_copy(a R):
    exist x R st {x = a}

witness $has_copy(2) from 2:
    2 = 2
